    <p data-i18n="s5.e5.p">The loop, closing in public: the engine mid-round, writing its own repository, while the
    operator watches the gates and touches nothing.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e6.h">xbgst-kimi walks its own repo: r14-3 → r14-5.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e6.p">The Kimi Code port ran the full self-orch protocol live on itself — three judged rounds,
    <b>43 accepted move groups shipped straight to main</b>. It caught its own guard rewrite
    introducing eleven bypass classes and rebuilt it segment-aware behind a 50-probe adversarial
    gate; when the guard ate the outside lane's audit prompt, the lane degraded locally and came
    back LIVE next round. After five rounds without convergence, <b>the honesty brake fired on its
    own</b> — the engine halted itself, on the record. The entry above said "still walking r14-3."
    It walked. It shipped. It stopped itself.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e7.h">64/64 — the concurrency ceiling, held live.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e7.p">From a lazy prompt with prior context, the port planned (wwkd), dispatched, judged, and
    shipped its own showcase: a ladder of 2/8/16/32 background-agent batches and a single 64-item
    swarm call — <b>64 completed, 64 distinct processes, full index coverage</b>, corroborated by
    the port's own audit hook. Same run, it patched itself: wave-mechanics doctrine for the host's
    swarm-exclusivity rule, a routing-check diagnostic that makes model pins legible, three new
    host-gap register rows. The kimi coding lane carried the entire ladder.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e8.h">xbgst-cursor joins the ports.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e8.p">A new L1 port lands: Cursor Agent CLI as a usage surface — probed overlay, pinned cursor-grok
    lane, PATH xask dry-fallback, no host mutation. Same orch, another CLI. Mapped under
    current art → L1 ports. <b>Private</b>, like the rest of the fleet-in-progress.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e9.h">Cursor Ultra × xask — priority L3 surface.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e9.p">Goal-oriented one-shot on <b>xbgst-cursor</b>: catalog adds <code>xask --provider cursor</code>
    with pinned CLI-ids (Grok/Composer/Kimi/OpenAI on Ultra). Alibaba Chinese models stay on
    <code>token-plan</code>. Orch FSD walk ships gates + charter evidence — evaluating Ultra as the
    priority xask lane for Cursor-native models. <span class="priv">private</span></p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e10.h">Kimi on a silver platter — the gravy train.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e10.p"><b>Kimi is the best model right now. Not even a question.</b>
    Grok bought Cursor and is handling it on a silver platter.
    Kimi is the side dish that is better than the main course.
    Kimi K3 Max fast inference via Cursor Ultra OAuth; PATH <code>xask --provider cursor --model-id kimi-k3-max</code>.
    Fast is Ultra metering, not a service-tier flag.
    The split holds: <b>xbgst-cursor-agent-surface</b> is the usage surface (grok-bot analog), not the judge;
    <b>xbgst-cursor</b> is the FSD orch port. Do not collapse them.
    Hangar cheap FIRST stays Token Plan. L1 crown stays xbgst on Grok Build — kimi is not crowned, cursor is not crowned.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">next</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e11.h">Kimi coding-model test battery.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e11.p">The kimi coding lane goes under load next. Find the walls. Document the failure modes. Failure is data.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e12.h">l1-elevate: xbgst-kimi takes the L1 substrate.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e12.p">The routing doctrine flipped: <b>k3-max</b> is now judge and session default, and every <b>K2.7</b>
    coding lane is pinned to the highspeed variant under the same managed OAuth provider. The
    <code>xask --provider cursor</code> ask-lane is live and unattended-verified, so Cursor-native models
    can draft without ever touching execution.</p>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e12.p2">Four workstreams are in flight: a kimi-flavored port of <b>xbgst-cursor-agent-surface</b>
    <span class="priv">private</span>, an A/B between the ported surface and the xask cursor lane,
    observe-only per-call telemetry for the speedrun <b>oauth tokenburn usage</b> category, and a
    <b>K2.7 thinking-off</b> variant probe. The cutover is real; the map is being rewritten underneath
    the operation.</p></div></div>

  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-08-24</span>
    <div><h3 data-i18n="s5.e13.h">l1-elevate closes: trust record live, telemetry feeding the board.</h3>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e13.p">The l1-elevate orchestration on <b>xbgst-kimi</b> closed the same day it opened. The trust
    decision record (<code>docs/CURSOR-TRUST.md</code>) now governs two cursor lane forms — a
    read-only xask consult lane and a scoped full-agent execution lane inside a dedicated,
    write-audited workspace. <b>xbgst-cursor-agent-surface</b> <span class="priv">private</span>
    is ported onto the kimi substrate; the A/B against the xask cursor lane ended
    <b>keep-both</b>: surface for correctness-critical execution, ask-lane for fast consults.</p>
    <p data-i18n="s5.e13.p2">Observe-only telemetry now records every cursor lane call, feeding the speedrun
    <b>oauth tokenburn usage</b> category. The K2.7 thinking-off probe closed
    <b>external-wontfix</b>. Pin discipline recalibrated: volume defaults to the included
    Cursor Models pool; kimi pins only when kimi-specific.</p></div></div>
  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-09-18</span>
    <div><h3>audit logs: 77 beats, every commit dispositioned.</h3>
    <p>Two fleet missions audited <code>ufo-fsd-alpha</code> end to end and the full trail ships in the <code>charterlogs</code> vault. <b>audit-early</b> — 53 beats, window <b>2026-08-24 → 2026-09-10</b>, from the empty root <code>f43c757c</code> to tip <code>5f52e03a</code>: all <b>1357</b> non-merge commits dispositioned per-SHA, zero unexplained. <b>audit-late</b> — 24 beats, window <b>2026-09-11 → 2026-09-18</b>, <code>9644f95a..4adedde6</code>: <b>34/34</b> window commits mapped to exactly one beat, zero gaps. Every beat carries an executable gate with observed output; both result reports ship with SHA256 receipts. Vault: <code>charterlogs/beats/</code> (77 beats) + <code>charterlogs/results/</code> (<code>audit-logs-early.md</code> · <code>audit-logs-late.md</code> + receipts). L1 = <b>Kimi K3 Max</b>; Gemini banned; parent goal OPEN.</p></div></div>
  <div class="logentry rv"><span class="ldate">2026-09-28</span>
    <div><h3>ufo-fsd-alpha sync: 4,523 commits from the Origin, mirrored and explained.</h3>
    <p>The charter synced with the writable Origin (<code>origin.cursor.com/jo-o-veiga/ufo-fsd-alpha</code>) through tip <code>ee907f1cf</code> (2026-09-27): <b>4,523 main-branch commits</b> since the audit-late window closed at <code>4adedde6</code> (2026-09-18), lineage verified by merge-base before writing. The arc: the <b>nightcall nc3 wave</b> (2026-09-20→22 — swarm mechanics, guidance re-pins, the canonical <code>/skill:ufo</code> invocation); the <b>nc5 wave</b> (2026-09-24 — the magga-2 OMP fleet on an isolated <code>$HOME</code>/<code>/scratch</code> clone with a bare audit-only <code>origin</code>, parent goal still OPEN, and <b>PrimeAgent assimilation</b> round 25: sha256-chained refinement ledger, typed compaction-digest API, read-only recovery advisory); the <b>nc6 synthesis</b> (72-lane verification round, findings absorbed and settled); the <b>magga fleet rounds</b> (2026-09-25→27 — orchestration/docs/crates/integration/verify/e2e, drill honesty passes, tracked-file census); the <b>nc8 super-wave</b> (2026-09-26→27 — a docs continuum sealed tip-bind by tip-bind plus runtime, verify, e2e, sighting, iterate, orchestration and substrate lanes, gate batteries green into the hundreds); closing with the <b>nc9 audit waves</b> (2026-09-27 — pin re-anchoring, falsified-claim repairs, judgment-binding parity, evidence probes green at 667) at tip <code>ee907f1cf</code>. Standing truth unchanged: dispatch stays <b>OMP-only</b>, <code>crates/ufo-core-runtime</code> is the sole judge, and two clauses remain open (authenticated live-LLM specialist evidence; the private LKG byte-identical clone). Parent goal OPEN. An earlier entry the same day cited a GitHub mirror (<code>VeigaPunk/ufo-fsd-alpha</code>) at 1,531 commits through <code>72ab84a8</code>; that mirror diverges from the Origin (its tip does not exist there) and has been retracted — <b>the Origin is the only source of record</b>.</p></div></div></section>


